# ============================================================================
# .gitattributes — what GitHub's automatic "Source code" archive carries
#
# Ian Howell — Embodied Music Lab — GPL-3.0-or-later
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR
#
# GitHub attaches a "Source code (zip)" asset to every release automatically.
# Nobody chooses its contents at release time; it is a `git archive` of the
# tag, and `git archive` honours `export-ignore` here. On 17 August 2026 that
# asset was 84 MB across 3,945 entries. The plugin was 327 of them. The other
# 3,435 were the GUI driving rig, its screenshots, and the audit record — a
# voice teacher who clicked the obvious link on the releases page downloaded
# 74 MB of harness and 24 MB of PNGs to get a 4.5 MB plugin.
#
# The lines at the bottom of this file cut that. Each one is justified there.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS FILE CANNOT DO — READ THIS BEFORE POINTING ANYONE AT THE ASSET
#
# `export-ignore` removes paths. That is the whole of its power. It CANNOT:
#
#   * rename anything. The archive's root is named from the tag
#     (EMLPraatTools-<tag>/) and every path beneath it keeps the name it has
#     in the tree. The plugin arrives as `plugin_EML_StatsGraphs/` because
#     THE SOURCE FOLDER IS CALLED THAT — a `git mv`, not a line in this file.
#   * set the install name. Same reason: Praat installs a plugin by the
#     folder's name, and the folder's name comes from the tree, not from here.
#   * carry a file mode. Git records the executable bit and nothing else, so
#     `git archive` writes every file 0644 — 0755 only where git says
#     executable — whatever its mode on disk. Nothing here changes that in
#     either direction: the modes in this asset are uniform because git
#     flattened them, not because anything checked them, and a mode defect in
#     a working tree is invisible from inside this archive. The instrument
#     that reads modes is `build-release.py --verify`, and it reads the built
#     folder and the zip.
#
# So what the asset offers is not built here — it is inherited from the tree.
# Measured 18 August 2026 on the archive these lines produce: 391 files, 1.9 MB
# zipped, and exactly ONE top-level entry that looks like a plugin —
# plugin_EML_StatsGraphs/, 257 files, every one of them 0644. Copied into a
# Praat preferences directory it registers the EML Stats & Graphs cascade and
# its commands open their dialogs, and the README quick start runs headless out
# of it and prints t(13.6) = 7.89, p < .001. v85 rebuilds that archive on every
# run of the suite and asserts the shape; harness/release/ drives the built zip.
#
# THE BUILT ZIP IS STILL THE RELEASE. plugin_EML_StatsGraphs/dev/tools/
# build-release.py produces the plugin folder alone, gates every file in it
# against RELEASE_EXCLUDE.tsv and the history rules, verifies the modes it set,
# and is what the documentation points at. This asset is that same folder with
# the licence, the README, the CI configuration and the validation suite beside
# it — things a user does not drag anywhere. The lines at the bottom of this
# file are what keep the folder they DO drag unambiguous: one candidate,
# wearing the name Praat installs it by.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHO THE ARCHIVE IS FOR
#
# Author ruling, 17 August 2026. Every remaining question about this file is
# a question about audience, and the two answers below land on OPPOSITE sides
# of it, so the audience is settled first and in one place rather than felt
# out one directory at a time.
#
# THIS ASSET IS FOR THE PERSON WHO CLICKED THE OBVIOUS LINK ON THE RELEASES
# PAGE BECAUSE THEY WANTED THE PLUGIN. It is not for a reader of the source.
# That is not a preference; the repository has already decided it three times
# over, and each of the three is measurable:
#
#   * A source reader CANNOT USE THIS ASSET AS SOURCE. Most validators read
#     evidence/ and several read audit/, and both are excluded below, so the
#     suite that ships here cannot be run from here. README.md and
#     validate/README.md already say "clone the repository" for that reason.
#   * The same is true one level down. RELEASE_EXCLUDE.tsv's own row says the
#     suites in plugin/dev/ "are run from a clone of this repository, against
#     plugin/ in place, and never from an installed copy" — so shipping them
#     inside the asset serves nobody who is actually running them either.
#   * The 84 MB measurement above exists because of a download in the OTHER
#     direction. Nobody has been observed fetching this asset to read the
#     design documents; the traffic it gets is people trying to install.
#
# So: where the two audiences pull against each other, the installer wins.
# Where they do not pull against each other, the reader is not charged
# anything. The next two sections are those two cases, and the difference
# between them is not the size of the directory — it is where the directory
# sits.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# validate/ SHIPS, plugin/dev/ DOES NOT, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS "BESIDE" vs
# "INSIDE"
#
# validate/ is the largest thing that stays after the plugin — 2.7 MB, roughly
# a quarter of the slimmed archive — and its absence would be the easy further
# saving. It stays: an archive calling itself "source" carries the tests that
# make the source credible, and for this project the validation suite is the
# product's main claim. v85 pins it — excluding it turns the suite red rather
# than making it smaller.
#
# plugin/dev/ is the same KIND of material — suites, tooling, design documents
# — and it goes. The two rulings are not in tension, because Praat installs a
# plugin BY DROPPING A FOLDER into its preferences directory. A user who
# unzips this asset and drags `plugin_EML_StatsGraphs/` into Praat carries
# EVERYTHING UNDER it and NOTHING BESIDE IT. dev/ is the only developer
# material in the repository that rides into the install: 1.4 MB and 72 files
# of test scaffolding, reference generators and release tooling, arriving
# inside the folder the user just installed. validate/, harness/ and audit/
# cannot do that, whatever they weigh.
#
# A reader loses nothing either way. plugin/dev/ is on github.com like the
# rest, and its suites require a clone regardless.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE RELATIONSHIP TO RELEASE_EXCLUDE.tsv — ONE SOURCE OF TRUTH
#
# There are two files in this repository that leave things out, and the
# repository has already been burned once this week by two hand-maintained
# lists drifting apart. The protection is not that they are kept apart — it is
# that ONE OF THEM IS THE AUTHOR AND THE OTHER MAY ONLY REPEAT IT:
#
#   RELEASE_EXCLUDE.tsv  is the sole AUTHOR of what leaves plugin/. It says
#                        what the installable artefact drops. Its paths are
#                        plugin-relative because the builder stages plugin/
#                        alone.
#   this file            is the sole author of what leaves the repository
#                        ROOT — those directories sit beside plugin/, which is
#                        exactly why RELEASE_EXCLUDE.tsv's header forbids
#                        naming them there ("an exclusion that can never
#                        match"). Inside plugin/ this file has NO OPINION OF
#                        ITS OWN. It may only restate a row that already
#                        exists in RELEASE_EXCLUDE.tsv.
#
# THE RULE THIS REPLACED, AND WHY. Until the audience ruling above, this file
# was forbidden to name any path under plugin/ at all, and the source archive
# was required to be a strict SUPERSET of the artefact inside plugin/ — on the
# reasoning that a source archive is for a source reader and plugin/dev/ is
# the first thing such a reader wants. That was a coherent rule for an archive
# aimed at a reader. It is the wrong rule for the asset this one turned out to
# be, and it is now:
#
#   (1) AN export-ignore LINE MAY NAME A PATH UNDER plugin/ ONLY IF
#       RELEASE_EXCLUDE.tsv ALREADY NAMES IT. This file may follow that list;
#       it may not extend it. Still one authority, still one place to read the
#       answer to "what does plugin/ drop".
#   (2) INSIDE plugin/, THE SOURCE ARCHIVE AND THE RELEASE ARTEFACT HOLD THE
#       SAME FILES. Not a superset — the same set. Every row of
#       RELEASE_EXCLUDE.tsv must be ABSENT from the archive, and every tracked
#       path under plugin/ that no row covers must be PRESENT.
#
# validate/v85 recomputes both of those from RELEASE_EXCLUDE.tsv as it is on
# disk at run time, so neither is restated here and neither can drift quietly.
#
# THE COUPLING THIS BUYS IS NAMED RATHER THAN HIDDEN. `git archive` cannot
# read a TSV, so a NEW row in RELEASE_EXCLUDE.tsv now needs a matching line
# here. That is a real cost and it is not left to anyone's memory: v85 fails
# on the next run and prints the path that is out of step. Loud is the
# property being bought. The old rule bought silence instead, by forbidding
# the overlap outright — and the price of that silence was 72 files of test
# scaffolding inside the folder a user drags into Praat.
# ============================================================================

# The audit record: findings logs, the D-numbered index, the static
# pre-GUI audits, the derived reports. 1.1 MB / 46 files. It is a record of
# work done ON the source, not source, and every path in it is readable on
# github.com by anyone who wants it.
audit/ export-ignore

# The GUI driving rig: Xvfb/xdotool harnesses, probes, per-harness fixtures
# and their committed output. 74.4 MB / 2,644 files — the single largest
# thing in the repository, and 88% of the old archive. It only functions
# inside a Linux sandbox with Praat, Xvfb, matchbox and ImageMagick standing;
# unzipped on a voice teacher's laptop it is inert weight.
harness/ export-ignore

# Raw artefacts the drive produced: 109 screenshots, 104 exported figures,
# the CSV and Info-window captures. 23.6 MB / 625 files. The validators read
# these from a CLONE, which is how the suite is meant to be run
# (`Rscript validate/run_all.R` from a checkout) — see validate/README.md.
# Consequence, stated plainly rather than discovered: the suite that ships in
# this archive CANNOT be run from this archive alone. Clone the repository to
# run it. Shipping 23.6 MB of PNGs to make a download self-executing is the
# wrong trade for an asset nobody should be installing from anyway.
evidence/ export-ignore

# A single-row incident log from an agent session (a harness that killed its
# own driving shell). Session bookkeeping about a rig that is itself excluded
# above.
RETRIES.tsv export-ignore

# One workstream's lane: 124 KB / 12 files — a report, a proposals document, a
# runner, and the committed red/green transcripts behind them. The same class
# as audit/ above, a record of work done ON the source rather than source, and
# judged the same way. What the lane produced already ships: validate/v90–v93
# came out of it, they sit in validate/ and they read their inputs from
# evidence/, so nothing in the asset loses a file it reads. Readable on
# github.com like the rest.
lane/ export-ignore

# The developer tree, and THE ONLY LINE IN THIS FILE THAT NAMES A PATH INSIDE
# the plugin folder. 1.4 MB / 72 files: the phase-1 and phase-2 Praat test
# suites, the Python and R reference generators, the release and manifest
# tooling, the four design documents, the retired experiments and the history
# ledger.
#
# It is here because RELEASE_EXCLUDE.tsv already names it — this line does not
# decide anything, it makes the source archive agree with the artefact (rule 2
# above), and v85 fails if the two ever disagree in either direction. What
# makes it worth the coupling is that plugin_EML_StatsGraphs/ is the folder a
# user drags into Praat's preferences directory: everything under it arrives
# in the install, and this is the only developer material in the repository
# that does. Readable on github.com; its own suites run from a clone.
plugin_EML_StatsGraphs/dev/ export-ignore

# THE COMPATIBILITY SYMLINK AT THE REPOSITORY ROOT. `plugin` is a symlink to
# plugin_EML_StatsGraphs, tracked by git as a 22-byte link. It exists so that
# the ~2,178 paths written as `plugin/...` across the harnesses, validators
# and documents keep resolving in a checkout; the alternative was editing all
# of them at once, and a rig whose path silently stops pointing at anything
# runs, measures nothing and reports success.
#
# IT MUST NOT REACH THE ARCHIVE. A user unzips this asset and drags ONE folder
# into Praat's preferences directory. Two entries that both look like the
# plugin — one of them a dangling link on any system that did not preserve it
# — is exactly the ambiguity the folder name was corrected to remove. The
# leading slash anchors this at the repository root, so it names the link and
# nothing else. v85 asserts the archive carries no top-level `plugin` entry.
/plugin export-ignore

# NOTHING BELOW THIS LINE. Any new entry must be justified in place, must
# either be outside plugin_EML_StatsGraphs/ or name a path RELEASE_EXCLUDE.tsv
# already names (rule 1 above), and must not be validate/ (the ruling above).
# v85 enforces all three against the archive it builds.
